WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.020
<v Narrator>Now let's discuss EC2 Launch types</v>

2
00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.280
and savings plans.

3
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.860
So we have On Demand Instances

4
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.330
and they're here for when you have short workloads

5
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.520
with predictable pricing

6
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.780
and they're going to be very reliable.

7
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.450
They're the kind of instances that you launch

8
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.800
when you first start on the console.

9
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.610
Spot Instances are going to be great

10
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.280
when you have very short workloads,

11
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.510
they're going to be cheap,

12
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.230
but you run the risk of losing these instances

13
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.590
because AWS can reclaim capacity

14
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.720
and therefore using Spot Instances

15
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.620
is not considered reliable.

16
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.320
So they're great when you need to have huge cost savings

17
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.320
and when your when your workload is resilient to failures.

18
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.960
Now if you go for long workloads,

19
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.910
you can go for the Reserved Instances.

20
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.580
And it's minimum one year that you need to commit.

21
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.670
And so you have different kinds;

22
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.390
you have the Reserved Instances

23
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.790
for long workloads,

24
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.250
you have the Convertible Reserved Instances

25
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.870
for long workloads where you have flexible instance types,

26
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.650
and then we'll see in the next slide,

27
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.940
we have something better called savings plan.

28
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.900
Then we have Dedicated Instances.

29
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.260
So, we have the guarantee with these instances

30
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.210
that no other customers will share your hardware

31
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.820
or will have Dedicated Hosts

32
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.530
where you book an entire physical server

33
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.690
and you also control instant placements within the server.

34
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.710
So these Dedicated Hosts have a use case.

35
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.810
This is great for software licenses

36
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.160
that will operate at the core or socket level

37
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.450
because you do have access to the core or circuits,

38
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.980
and then you can also define host affinity

39
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.570
so that the instance reboots are kept on the same hosts.

40
00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.970
Okay, so that's for the EC2 Easy to Launch types.

41
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.540
Then for the savings plan,

42
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.900
this is a new pricing model to get discounts

43
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.220
based on long-term usage.

44
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.240
So I think over time,

45
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.780
this is going to replace reserve instances.

46
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.590
So the idea is that you're going to commit

47
00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:42.810
to a certain type of usage.

48
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.667
For example, you say,

49
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.170
"hey, I will spend $10 per hour

50
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.200
for the next 1 to 3 years."

51
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.360
And then any usage beyond the savings plan

52
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.330
is going to be billed at the on-demand price,

53
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.610
but then beforehand, you are doing these savings.

54
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.200
So you have the EC2 instance Saving Plans

55
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.050
which gives you up to 72% discount

56
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.410
which is the same discount as standard RIs,

57
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.600
but it's more flexible because you have to-

58
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.220
You can select the instance family,

59
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.970
so M5, C5, and so on,

60
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.710
and you're locked to a specific region

61
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.480
and it's going to be flexible across size.

62
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.020
So it could be an M5 large and m5.4xlarge and so on.

63
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.370
You can get any OS you want,

64
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.430
so Windows, Linux,

65
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.763
and then the tenancy can be either dedicated or default.

66
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.330
Then you get a Compute Savings plan.

67
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:27.410
So it's 66%, which is the same as the convertible RIs,

68
00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.060
and with these, you get the ability

69
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.760
to move between instance families.

70
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.750
So you can have a C5 and then have an M5.

71
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.160
You can move across regions.

72
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.020
So you can go from Ireland to the US,

73
00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.930
or you can have different compute types.

74
00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.690
So it could be for EC2 instances,

75
00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.520
for Fargate, for Lambda,

76
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.550
and you can change the OS

77
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.150
and you can change the tenancy.

78
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.260
So they're the most flexible obviously

79
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.170
which is why they're a little bit less intense

80
00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.280
in terms of savings, but they're great.

81
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.320
And then finally, there's the SageMaker Savings Plan

82
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.970
which allows you to save up to 64% off

83
00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.040
on your SageMaker workloads.

84
00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.350
All right, let's say for this lecture, I hope you liked it

85
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.300
and I will see you in the next lecture.

